1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,725
The Bank of China Building on Battery Road

2
00:00:03,725 --> 00:00:09,055
In May 1937, the Bank of China
paid $715,000 for one of the most

3
00:00:09,135 --> 00:00:14,041
valuable properties in Singapore
— a marble-fronted banking

4
00:00:14,121 --> 00:00:19,700
palace on Battery Road — and then,
for ten years, did not move in.

5
00:00:19,700 --> 00:00:23,670
The palace stayed a department
store, the bank stayed down

6
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:27,580
the road in Cecil Street, and
when the bank finally took

7
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:31,850
possession in 1947 it was only
to pull the whole thing down.

8
00:00:31,850 --> 00:00:34,909
The building the Bank
of China is known by in

9
00:00:34,989 --> 00:00:38,825
Singapore is the tower it
built on the site afterwards.

10
00:00:38,825 --> 00:00:41,404
The palace it bought, and never used,

11
00:00:41,484 --> 00:00:44,000
is the forgotten half of the story.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,950
The palace had been built
for another bank entirely.

13
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:50,718
In December 1893 the
Chartered Bank of India,

14
00:00:50,798 --> 00:00:55,845
Australia and China leased a plot at the
corner of Battery Road and Flint Street,

15
00:00:55,925 --> 00:00:58,820
on a site cleared by a
fire that July which had

16
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:02,618
burned the godowns of McAlister
& Co. and McKerrow & Co. The

17
00:01:02,698 --> 00:01:06,226
architects were Swan & Maclaren
— the design seems likely

18
00:01:06,306 --> 00:01:09,834
to have been the work of James
Meikle, who left Singapore

19
00:01:09,914 --> 00:01:13,775
before it was finished — and
the building cost about $60,000.

20
00:01:13,775 --> 00:01:18,575
The bank moved in on the
6th of February 1895.

21
00:01:18,575 --> 00:01:22,232
It was a palazzo in the full
nineteenth-century sense:

22
00:01:22,312 --> 00:01:27,285
three storeys and about sixty feet
tall, rusticated up to the first-floor

23
00:01:27,365 --> 00:01:31,784
cornice on a six-foot granite
base, its upper floors divided into

24
00:01:31,864 --> 00:01:36,421
five bays by two-storey Corinthian
pilasters, with a heavy cornice,

25
00:01:36,501 --> 00:01:41,000
a balustraded parapet and a row
of ornamental urns along the top.

26
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,573
Inside was a banking hall nineteen
feet high with a semi-circular

27
00:01:45,653 --> 00:01:49,296
counter and an encaustic
tile floor, and a fireproof

28
00:01:49,376 --> 00:01:53,600
bullion strongroom walled in
granite and concrete-and-iron.

29
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:56,802
Readers of our post on the
exchange banks of Raffles

30
00:01:56,882 --> 00:02:00,147
Place will have met it there
— it was the Chartered's

31
00:02:00,227 --> 00:02:03,825
grand statement on the
waterfront, built to be looked at.

32
00:02:03,825 --> 00:02:07,800
The Chartered itself only
stayed twenty-one years.

33
00:02:07,800 --> 00:02:12,396
Having outgrown the palazzo, it
built Chartered Bank Chambers

34
00:02:12,476 --> 00:02:17,075
further along the road and moved
out on the 6th of May 1916.

35
00:02:17,075 --> 00:02:20,133
The Bank of Taiwan took
the ground floor and

36
00:02:20,213 --> 00:02:22,772
Mitsui Bussan Kaisha the floor above,

37
00:02:22,852 --> 00:02:26,775
and the building settled into
a second life as offices.

38
00:02:26,775 --> 00:02:31,402
When the Bank of Taiwan left
for Raffles Place in May 1931,

39
00:02:31,482 --> 00:02:36,668
the palace stood vacant into 1933,
was dressed up by the architect

40
00:02:36,748 --> 00:02:41,375
Heinrich Rudolf Arbenz, and
reopened as "Publicity House".

41
00:02:41,375 --> 00:02:45,889
Then, on the 8th of October
1934, the Kuala Lumpur department

42
00:02:45,969 --> 00:02:50,785
store Gian Singh & Co. opened a
Singapore branch in its basement,

43
00:02:50,865 --> 00:02:54,776
run by brothers Hardial
Singh Bajaj and Pritam Singh,

44
00:02:54,856 --> 00:02:59,375
with the retailer M. S. Ally &
Co. as the other main tenant.

45
00:02:59,375 --> 00:03:03,425
The Chartered Bank's bullion
palace had become a shop.

46
00:03:03,425 --> 00:03:07,925
The Bank of China arrived in
Singapore by a different door.

47
00:03:07,925 --> 00:03:13,129
Founded in 1912, the bank had set
up its overseas branches largely

48
00:03:13,209 --> 00:03:18,492
to handle the remittances and
foreign exchange of overseas Chinese,

49
00:03:18,572 --> 00:03:22,975
and its head office planned
a Singapore branch in 1935.

50
00:03:22,975 --> 00:03:26,316
The branch opened on
the 15th of June 1936 —

51
00:03:26,396 --> 00:03:30,125
in the lower two floors
of the Great Eastern Life

52
00:03:30,205 --> 00:03:34,325
building in Cecil Street,
not on Battery Road at all.

53
00:03:34,325 --> 00:03:38,334
The bank's own history records
an opening of some ceremony:

54
00:03:38,414 --> 00:03:41,591
more than a thousand
guests, and nine Royal Air

55
00:03:41,671 --> 00:03:45,275
Force planes flying three
circles over the building.

56
00:03:45,275 --> 00:03:48,125
A year later came the purchase.

57
00:03:48,125 --> 00:03:53,047
In May 1937 the Straits Times
announced the $715,000 land deal:

58
00:03:53,128 --> 00:03:57,256
the bank's Cecil Street
rooms were growing too small,

59
00:03:57,336 --> 00:04:00,512
and it intended, in time, to pull the old

60
00:04:00,592 --> 00:04:04,800
building down and build its
own premises on the site.

61
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:08,396
In the meantime the property
— by then numbered 4–6

62
00:04:08,476 --> 00:04:12,225
Battery Road — took the
name Bank of China Building.

63
00:04:12,225 --> 00:04:15,525
It was a name on a deed, nothing more.

64
00:04:15,525 --> 00:04:18,674
The tenants' lease had
about four years to run,

65
00:04:18,754 --> 00:04:22,247
and the palace traded on
as a department store under

66
00:04:22,327 --> 00:04:25,900
its new owner's name above
the door in all but fact.

67
00:04:25,900 --> 00:04:29,900
The war of the lease came first, in 1940.

68
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:34,312
That October the bank applied
to the Rent Assessment Board

69
00:04:34,392 --> 00:04:39,501
to evict Gian Singh and M. S. Ally
so that it could move in itself:

70
00:04:39,581 --> 00:04:42,289
its staff had quadrupled since 1936,

71
00:04:42,369 --> 00:04:47,325
and its Cecil Street lease would
expire on the 31st of May 1941.

72
00:04:47,325 --> 00:04:50,300
The applications were refused.

73
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:55,603
The board allowed a fresh application
against Gian Singh the following March,

74
00:04:55,683 --> 00:04:58,400
but in the event the only tenant put out

75
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:02,675
of the Bank of China Building
in 1940 was a cigarette stall.

76
00:05:02,675 --> 00:05:05,225
Then came the larger war.

77
00:05:05,225 --> 00:05:09,542
The bank's Singapore branch
closed as Japanese forces overran

78
00:05:09,622 --> 00:05:14,659
Southeast Asia in 1942 — only its
London, New York, Calcutta and Bombay

79
00:05:14,739 --> 00:05:19,056
offices stayed open — while back
on Battery Road, remarkably,

80
00:05:19,136 --> 00:05:24,029
both department stores traded on
through the Occupation; by July 1943

81
00:05:24,109 --> 00:05:28,650
a Japanese restaurant, the Mutsumi,
had opened in the building.

82
00:05:28,650 --> 00:05:33,639
After the war the palace was
requisitioned by the British Army,

83
00:05:33,719 --> 00:05:38,225
which did not vacate it until
the 22nd of November 1946.

84
00:05:38,225 --> 00:05:40,688
What followed was reported in the

85
00:05:40,768 --> 00:05:43,850
newspapers as Singapore's "squat" drama.

86
00:05:43,850 --> 00:05:48,379
The bank gave both stores
until the 15th of January

87
00:05:48,459 --> 00:05:52,525
1947 to leave, meaning
to demolish and build.

88
00:05:52,525 --> 00:05:56,471
The Rent Board refused an
extension; on the day itself the

89
00:05:56,551 --> 00:06:00,290
two firms simply stayed put,
even after their water and

90
00:06:00,370 --> 00:06:05,704
electricity were cut — the supplies were
restored on appeal to Lazarus Rayman,

91
00:06:05,784 --> 00:06:08,700
the president of the Municipal Commission.

92
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:12,027
Gian Singh's troubles
compounded when goods

93
00:06:12,107 --> 00:06:15,751
worth $30,000 were stolen
from its new premises

94
00:06:15,831 --> 00:06:19,475
in a dawn robbery the
day before the deadline.

95
00:06:19,475 --> 00:06:22,675
The bank sued both firms in February.

96
00:06:22,675 --> 00:06:26,321
M. S. Ally moved out by
the 10th of February;

97
00:06:26,401 --> 00:06:30,294
Gian Singh finally followed
once its new Raffles

98
00:06:30,374 --> 00:06:34,100
Place store opened on
the 15th of March 1947.

99
00:06:34,100 --> 00:06:37,595
Demolition of the palazzo
was under way by May,

100
00:06:37,675 --> 00:06:42,158
under a Malaya Tribune headline
that already looked past it:

101
00:06:42,238 --> 00:06:44,900
"Work Begins on S'pore Skyscraper".

102
00:06:44,900 --> 00:06:48,373
The bank had lodged its
plans the previous November:

103
00:06:48,453 --> 00:06:52,882
a fourteen-storey building, 200
feet high, which the Straits Times

104
00:06:52,962 --> 00:06:56,925
said would be the tallest
building in Malaya if completed.

105
00:06:56,925 --> 00:07:00,944
Foundation work began in
November 1948 and went slowly,

106
00:07:01,024 --> 00:07:05,341
out of care for the foundations
of the buildings next door;

107
00:07:05,421 --> 00:07:09,291
scientists pronounced the
work safe in February 1950,

108
00:07:09,371 --> 00:07:12,725
and the foundations were
finished that month.

109
00:07:12,725 --> 00:07:16,850
Then the building produced
its most dated document.

110
00:07:16,850 --> 00:07:21,692
With the foundations complete, the
branch sought — and, in May 1950,

111
00:07:21,772 --> 00:07:26,550
obtained — its head office's
approval to build the superstructure.

112
00:07:26,550 --> 00:07:30,681
The head office was now in
Peking: the Bank of China had come

113
00:07:30,761 --> 00:07:34,824
under the new government in
China in 1949, and Singapore was

114
00:07:34,904 --> 00:07:38,137
among the branches in
Hong Kong, London, Penang,

115
00:07:38,217 --> 00:07:43,050
Kuala Lumpur and elsewhere that
joined the Beijing-headquartered bank.

116
00:07:43,050 --> 00:07:47,178
A Singapore building permit,
countersigned in a new capital,

117
00:07:47,258 --> 00:07:50,975
is the revolution arriving
in the colony's paperwork.

118
00:07:50,975 --> 00:07:56,696
Tenders were called in March 1951
and construction began that April,

119
00:07:56,776 --> 00:08:00,700
at a cost the Straits
Times put at $3 million.

120
00:08:00,700 --> 00:08:04,139
The architects were Palmer
and Turner of Hong Kong,

121
00:08:04,219 --> 00:08:09,325
who were building the Bank of China's
Hong Kong premises at the same time.

122
00:08:09,325 --> 00:08:12,279
The block was finished in December 1953,

123
00:08:12,359 --> 00:08:16,906
and on the 28th of December the
Singapore Standard announced:

124
00:08:16,986 --> 00:08:20,475
"Bank Of China Moves
Into New Building Today."

125
00:08:20,475 --> 00:08:23,985
Seventeen years after it
opened in Cecil Street,

126
00:08:24,065 --> 00:08:29,450
and sixteen after buying the site,
the bank was finally on Battery Road.

127
00:08:29,450 --> 00:08:33,141
The Standard described a block
designed to look like the

128
00:08:33,221 --> 00:08:37,113
bank's premises in Shanghai
and Hong Kong: strict symmetry,

129
00:08:37,193 --> 00:08:41,220
plaster finish, metal-framed
windows, with deliberate Chinese

130
00:08:41,300 --> 00:08:45,475
touches in the oriental trimmings
and mouldings of the façade.

131
00:08:45,475 --> 00:08:49,414
The bank took the lowest three
and the top three floors;

132
00:08:49,494 --> 00:08:53,225
the basement held safe-deposit
boxes and a car park.

133
00:08:53,225 --> 00:08:56,616
It was a building of firsts
— the first in Singapore to

134
00:08:56,696 --> 00:09:00,275
incorporate a mail chute and
document lifts, and the first

135
00:09:00,355 --> 00:09:03,241
in the country to be
centrally air-conditioned,

136
00:09:03,321 --> 00:09:07,675
though at the opening only the lower
half of the building was served.

137
00:09:07,675 --> 00:09:12,588
And at the Battery Road entrance
stood the detail that has outlasted

138
00:09:12,668 --> 00:09:16,846
every argument about the
building: a pair of Chinese lions

139
00:09:16,926 --> 00:09:21,625
in pre-cast stone, sculpted by
the Italian artist Rodolfo Nolli.

140
00:09:21,625 --> 00:09:24,393
They were among his
last works in Singapore,

141
00:09:24,473 --> 00:09:27,450
and they are still on
guard at the door today.

142
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:31,833
About the tower's height, the
record needs one careful paragraph,

143
00:09:31,913 --> 00:09:33,756
because the claim most often

144
00:09:33,836 --> 00:09:37,475
repeated about it does not
survive its own footnotes.

145
00:09:37,475 --> 00:09:41,290
The building stands 200 feet
(61 metres), and the National

146
00:09:41,370 --> 00:09:46,931
Heritage Board calls it, fairly, "one of
the first modern skyscrapers in Singapore";

147
00:09:47,011 --> 00:09:49,752
on the low-rise Battery Road waterfront it

148
00:09:49,832 --> 00:09:53,848
towered over everything, and a
period postcard could describe

149
00:09:53,928 --> 00:09:58,025
it without embarrassment as the
tallest building of its time.

150
00:09:58,025 --> 00:10:01,725
But it was never the tallest
building in Singapore.

151
00:10:01,725 --> 00:10:06,076
The Cathay Building, finished
in 1939, already stood taller

152
00:10:06,156 --> 00:10:10,283
at 70 metres, and within two
years of the bank's opening

153
00:10:10,363 --> 00:10:14,414
the Asia Insurance Building,
a few hundred metres away,

154
00:10:14,494 --> 00:10:18,775
rose to 82 metres and took
the national title for itself.

155
00:10:18,775 --> 00:10:21,795
What the Bank of China
Building genuinely was —

156
00:10:21,875 --> 00:10:26,808
the tallest building on the Singapore
River waterfront, and for a generation

157
00:10:26,888 --> 00:10:30,450
the landmark of Raffles Place
— is distinction enough.

158
00:10:30,450 --> 00:10:33,758
The branch's hardest
test came a decade later,

159
00:10:33,838 --> 00:10:37,225
and it came from politics
rather than banking.

160
00:10:37,225 --> 00:10:40,941
In the Federation of Malaya,
the bank's branches had

161
00:10:41,021 --> 00:10:44,955
already been made to close
in 1959, under a banking law

162
00:10:45,035 --> 00:10:48,678
that barred banks under
foreign government control;

163
00:10:48,758 --> 00:10:52,700
its Kuala Lumpur building
passed to the Bank of Tokyo.

164
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:57,762
Singapore, a separate jurisdiction,
was unaffected — until December 1964,

165
00:10:57,842 --> 00:11:01,988
in the middle of a revenue
dispute between Singapore and the

166
00:11:02,068 --> 00:11:06,778
federal government in Kuala Lumpur,
when Malaysia's finance minister

167
00:11:06,858 --> 00:11:11,225
announced that the Bank of China
in Singapore would be closed.

168
00:11:11,225 --> 00:11:15,412
The branch handled the financial
arrangements for Singapore's

169
00:11:15,492 --> 00:11:19,609
trade and remittances with
China, and Singapore's government

170
00:11:19,689 --> 00:11:24,016
said no. "S'pore Govt Opposes
Closure of Bank," ran the Straits

171
00:11:24,096 --> 00:11:27,094
Times headline on the
31st of December 1964;

172
00:11:27,174 --> 00:11:32,350
the Minister for Culture, S. Rajaratnam,
criticised the decision publicly.

173
00:11:32,350 --> 00:11:35,500
The closure was never carried out.

174
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:38,934
When Singapore became
independent in August 1965,

175
00:11:39,014 --> 00:11:44,240
its government decided the branch would
be allowed to continue operating —

176
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:48,614
a decision the bank publicly
welcomed as good for Singapore's

177
00:11:48,694 --> 00:11:52,925
economy and for friendly
relations between the two peoples.

178
00:11:52,925 --> 00:11:58,100
The tower on Battery Road had
survived its second change of flags.

179
00:11:58,100 --> 00:12:01,275
The building today is two buildings.

180
00:12:01,275 --> 00:12:06,159
In December 1990 the bank disclosed
plans for a $90 million extension;

181
00:12:06,239 --> 00:12:11,336
the ground was broken on the 15th of
April 1994, and in 2000 a new tower,

182
00:12:11,416 --> 00:12:15,449
168 metres tall, was completed
immediately beside the 1953

183
00:12:15,529 --> 00:12:19,917
block on a shared podium — designed
by Palmer and Turner again,

184
00:12:19,997 --> 00:12:25,378
nearly half a century on, deliberately
inheriting the older building's style,

185
00:12:25,458 --> 00:12:29,500
with its rising lines and
geometrised Chinese decoration.

186
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:32,965
The old block still stands
within the development,

187
00:12:33,045 --> 00:12:38,212
still the bank's Singapore headquarters
and main branch at 4 Battery Road,

188
00:12:38,292 --> 00:12:42,253
its upper floors let as offices;
in April 2015 the branch

189
00:12:42,333 --> 00:12:45,586
opened a historical
gallery on its first floor,

190
00:12:45,666 --> 00:12:50,700
the first such exhibition mounted by
any Bank of China overseas branch.

191
00:12:50,700 --> 00:12:53,800
The lions remain at the entrance.

192
00:12:53,800 --> 00:12:58,036
For all that, the 1953 block
holds no conservation status —

193
00:12:58,116 --> 00:13:02,937
unlike the Asia Insurance Building,
gazetted in 2007 as Singapore's

194
00:13:03,017 --> 00:13:07,399
first protected skyscraper, the
Bank of China Building stands

195
00:13:07,479 --> 00:13:12,666
in the middle of the financial district
unlisted and largely unremarked,

196
00:13:12,746 --> 00:13:16,550
a first-generation skyscraper
hiding in plain sight.

197
00:13:16,550 --> 00:13:19,768
Where it fits in the bigger
story: This post began

198
00:13:19,848 --> 00:13:23,329
as a follow-up to the exchange
banks of Raffles Place,

199
00:13:23,409 --> 00:13:26,575
where this plot was the
Chartered Bank's palace.

200
00:13:26,575 --> 00:13:30,717
Its second life is the stranger
one: a site bought in 1937 as

201
00:13:30,797 --> 00:13:34,663
an option on the future, held
through a refused eviction,

202
00:13:34,743 --> 00:13:38,955
an occupation and a tenants'
sit-in, until the purchaser could

203
00:13:39,035 --> 00:13:43,246
build on it; a branch that closed
for a war, took its building

204
00:13:43,326 --> 00:13:47,053
orders from a new capital in
1950, and was kept open in

205
00:13:47,133 --> 00:13:51,425
1965 by a brand-new country's
government deciding it belonged.

206
00:13:51,425 --> 00:13:55,285
Battery Road has been rebuilt
many times over, but few of

207
00:13:55,365 --> 00:13:59,086
its corners carry the whole
arc — colonial bank palace,

208
00:13:59,166 --> 00:14:03,175
department store, squat,
skyscraper — in a single address.
